# STRING(3)

## NAME

strcat, strncat, strcmp, strncmp, strcpy, strncpy, strlen, strchr, strrchr, strstr, strtok,
strdup, index, rindex, memcpy, memmove, memset, memcmp, memchr - string and memory operations

## SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <string.h>

char *strcat(char *s1, const char *s2);

char *strncat(char *s1, const char *s2, size_t n);

int strcmp(const char *s1, const char *s2);

int strncmp(const char *s1, const char *s2, size_t n);

char *strcpy(char *s1, const char *s2);

char *strncpy(char *s1, const char *s2, size_t n);

size_t strlen(const char *s);

char *strchr(const char *s, int c);

char *strrchr(const char *s, int c);

char *strstr(const char *s, const char *sub);

char *strtok(char *s, const char *sep);

char *strdup(const char *s);

char *index(const char *s, int c);

char *rindex(const char *s, int c);

void *memcpy(void *d, const void *s, size_t n);

void *memmove(void *d, const void *s, size_t n);

void *memset(void *d, int c, size_t n);

int memcmp(const void *a, const void *b, size_t n);

void *memchr(const void *s, int c, size_t n);
```

## DESCRIPTION

These functions operate on null-terminated strings. They do not check for overflow of any
receiving string.

*Strcat* appends a copy of string *s2* to the end of string *s1*. *Strncat* copies at most *n*
characters. Both return a pointer to the null-terminated result.

*Strcmp* compares its arguments and returns an integer greater than, equal to, or less than 0,
according as *s1* is lexicographically greater than, equal to, or less than *s2*. *Strncmp*
makes the same comparison but looks at at most *n* characters.

*Strcpy* copies string *s2* to *s1,* stopping after the null character has been moved.
*Strncpy* copies exactly *n* characters, truncating or null-padding *s2;* the target may not be
null-terminated if the length of *s2* is *n* or more. Both return *s1*.

*Strlen* returns the number of non-null characters in *s*.

*Index* (*rindex*) returns a pointer to the first (last) occurrence of character *c* in string
*s,* or zero if *c* does not occur in the string.

**Note:** everything from here down is C11 and is not on v7's page. *Strchr* and *strrchr* are
the standard spellings of *index* and *rindex*, and all four are present. *Strstr* returns a
pointer to the first occurrence of the string *sub* in *s*, or NULL. *Strtok* splits *s* into
tokens separated by any character of *sep*, returning one per call - the first call names the
string, the rest pass NULL - and it writes on the string it is given.

**Note:** *strdup* is neither v7's nor C11's - it is POSIX's, and C took it only in C23. It
returns a malloc(3)ed copy of *s*, which the caller frees, or NULL if the allocation failed. It
is the one routine on this page that allocates, and therefore the one that can fail.

*Memcpy* copies *n* bytes, *memmove* does the same for overlapping regions, *memset* fills *n*
bytes with *c*, *memcmp* compares *n* bytes, and *memchr* finds the first *c* in *n* bytes.

**Note:** <string.h> declares all of these, including *index* and *rindex*. v7 declared none of
them anywhere and left them to K&R's implicit **int**, which would have made *strcpy* return an
integer.

## SEE ALSO

strtol(3), atof(3), malloc(3) for *strdup's* storage

## BUGS

**Note:** v7's bug - "strcmp uses native character comparison, which is signed on PDP11's,
unsigned on other machines" - is settled here rather than left open. **char is unsigned,** so
the comparison is unsigned, and *strcmp* of two strings differing above 0177 orders them by
their byte values.

## BESM-6 NOTES

### These are the c-compiler's, not v7's

The whole *str\**/*mem\** family and atoi(3) are taken from the external c-compiler's own libc
rather than ported from v7, because they were already written for this machine's fat **char
\*** and one-word scalars, and their signatures already match the <string.h> this tree supplies
\- so nothing ends up defined as **int n** where the declaration says **size_t**. Each file says
at its head where it came from, and a divergence has to be written down there.

### A relational between two char \* is safe here, and it is used

A fat pointer does *not* sort as a plain word - incrementing one *decreases* its 3-bit byte
offset, which sits above the word address - so a raw word compare of two pointers into the same
word comes out backwards.

**The compiler knows.** It lowers a relational between two pointers it can see are fat through
**b$pdiff**, the same helper as **-**, and tests the sign. *Memmove*'s direction test depends
on that, and so does qsort(3)'s partition. lib/test/strings overlaps its arguments *within one
word* on purpose, to keep it that way.

**That is not a general licence.** Where the comparison is between a pointer and something the
front end does not carry as a fat pointer, it silently reduces to an integer comparison of the
whole word and the byte offset dominates. See getpass(3), where exactly that made the routine
return the empty string every time.

### strlen returns a signed size_t

**size_t** is signed here, a deliberate departure from C11 sec 7.19 recorded in
include/README.md, so a difference of two lengths behaves in a mixed comparison.

